      "time": "",
      "narrative": "Tab 1\n\nObserve isn't only about one agent in Foundry. It's also about understanding how the whole fleet is governed when it runs across the business.\n\nThat is Agent 365, which is where Observability lands.\n\nLet’s start in the registry. Here I can see every single one of my agents across my agentic estate. As a developer, I have a lot of options for what I can do and see here… For example, the contract policy expert that Jessica was working on has surfaced all the way here in the governance layer.\n\nI want to see which agents are being requested for review,\n\n*<click into 2nd tab>*",
      "demo_actions": "",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "",
      "narrative": "Tab 2\n\nwhich I can do in this Requests tab. Here are three agents pending review, let’s dig into the Staffing Agent.\n\nI can see what data and tools it has, any security, as well as its permissions, which as I scroll down, I see it’s using Mail ReadWrite and Send permissions, which I’ve explicitly told developers not to enable. I’m going to reject this submission.\n\nNow let’s check out the Product Backlog Agent. This one passed review and it’s time to publish to the store. As I do that, I have the option to publish this agent to specific users or groups. As you can see, it’s not just about putting agents out there, it’s about deploying them to the users who need them.\n\nLast but not least, observability would be incomplete without being able to see how much this is costing us.\n\n*<click into 3rd tab>*",
      "demo_actions": "",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
